# Churn scorecard

The course deliverable "churn scorecard" has two forms in this project:

1. **Customer scores:** every customer's calibrated churn probability and risk band ([scorecard table](../reports/tables/scorecard.csv)); the targeting tables add value, offer, expected value and the reason for each decision.
2. **Points card (this page):** the deployed model rewritten as whole points, so a call-centre agent or CRM rule can score a customer without the model and see why the score is high.

{% if meta.available %}
## How to read it

- Start from **{{ meta.base_points }} points** and add the points for each of the customer's attributes.
- **{{ meta.anchor_score }} points = 50% churn probability.** Every **{{ meta.pdo }} points doubles the odds** of churning; {{ meta.pdo }} points fewer halves them.
- Higher means riskier. The points come from the calibrated {{ model.label }} (calibration folded in); tenure and monthly charges are banded.
- On the {{ data.splits.test | num }} test customers the card's probability is within **{{ (meta.mean_abs_probability_gap * 100) | num(1) }} percentage points** of the model's on average (largest gap {{ (meta.max_abs_probability_gap * 100) | num(1) }} points; validation {{ (meta.validation_mean_abs_probability_gap * 100) | num(1) }}). It ranks customers as well as the model (ROC-AUC {{ meta.card_roc_auc | num(3) }} vs {{ meta.model_roc_auc | num(3) }}).

Points measure churn risk only. Whether to make an offer also depends on the customer's value and the offer's cost; use the targeting list for that.

